{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '861,1140p' GUIDE.md\"","aggregated_output":"// Look Lab: one scene, six looks. Keys 1-6 pick a look, T toggles chunky pixels on the custom\n// one. Walk around (WASD): characters & objects (the 'actors' layer) and the environment each\n// get their own look.\nimport { compileClips, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, LOOK_PRESETS, mergeStaticMeshes, PlatformerCharacter, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\n\nconst { BoxGeometry, Mesh, Vector3 } = THREE;\n\n// A look of our own: pixel-art characters on a clean, cel-shaded world, a vignette on top.\nconst STORYBOOK_HEROES = {\n  scene: [{ id: 'vignette', params: { intensity: 0.35 } }],\n  actors: { pixel: { size: 1, outline: 0.85, crease: 0.2 }, filters: [{ id: 'nes' }] },\n  environment: { pixel: null, filters: [{ id: 'cel', params: { bands: 4, ink: 0.6 } }] },\n};\nconst LOOKS = [\n  ['default', LOOK_PRESETS.none],\n  ['storybook heroes', STORYBOOK_HEROES],\n  ['pixel heroes', LOOK_PRESETS.pixel_heroes],\n  ['noir', LOOK_PRESETS.noir],\n  ['sin city', LOOK_PRESETS.sin_city],\n  ['handheld', LOOK_PRESETS.handheld],\n];\n\nclass LookLab {\n  name = 'Look Lab';\n  assets = [HERO_MODEL, 'assets/coin.glb', 'assets/tree.glb'];\n  current = 1;\n  target = new Vector3();\n\n  async setup(ctx) {\n    const { scene, physics, palette } = ctx;\n    const parts = [];\n    for (const [x, z, w, h, d, c] of [[0, 0, 16, 1, 12, 'green'], [-4, -3, 2, 2, 2, 'slate'], [4, -3, 3, 1, 2, 'sand'], [0, -5, 6, 3, 1, 'plum']]) {\n      const y = c === 'green' ? -0.5 : h / 2;\n      physics.addStaticBox({ position: [x, y, z], halfExtents: [w / 2, h / 2, d / 2] });\n      const m = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(palette[c]));\n      m.position.set(x, y, z);\n      m.castShadow = m.receiveShadow = true;\n      parts.push(m);\n    }\n    scene.add(...mergeStaticMeshes(parts));\n    for (const [x, z] of [[-6, 3], [6, 2], [-2, -4]]) {\n      const tree = await ctx.loadModel('assets/tree.glb'); // environment: no layer tag\n      tree.scene.position.set(x, 0, z);\n      scene.add(tree.scene);\n    }\n    // Objects on the actors layer: coins and a crate.\n    const coin = await ctx.loadModel('assets/coin.glb');\n    this.coins = [[-1.5, 0.6, 1], [0, 0.6, 1], [1.5, 0.6, 1]].map(([x, y, z]) => {\n      const c = coin.scene.clone(true);\n      c.position.set(x, y, z);\n      setLookLayer(c, 'actors');\n      scene.add(c);\n      return c;\n    });\n    const crate = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(1, 1, 1), toonMaterial(palette.orange));\n    crate.position.set(3, 0.5, 2);\n    crate.castShadow = true;\n    setLookLayer(crate, 'actors');\n    scene.add(crate);\n    physics.addStaticBox({ position: [3, 0.5, 2], halfExtents: [0.5, 0.5, 0.5] });\n\n    const hero = await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL);\n    this.model = hero.scene;\n    setLookLayer(this.model, 'actors');\n    scene.add(this.model);\n    this.hero = new PlatformerCharacter(physics, { position: [0, 0, 3], lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth });\n    this.hero.attachModel(this.model, compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, this.model), HERO_RIG);\n  }\n\n  fixedUpdate(ctx, dt) {\n    this.input = readMoveInput(ctx, this.hero, this.input);\n    this.hero.fixedUpdate(dt, this.input);\n  }\n\n  update(ctx, dt) {\n    this.hero.updateVisual(this.model, dt, ctx.physics.alpha);\n    for (const c of this.coins) c.rotation.y += dt * 2;\n    for (let i = 0; i < LOOKS.length; i++) {\n      if (ctx.input.wasPressed(`Digit${i + 1}`)) {\n        this.current = i;\n        ctx.engine.setLook(LOOKS[i][1]);\n      }\n    }\n    if (ctx.input.wasPressed('KeyT')) {\n      // Parameters and pixel sizes are uniforms: changing them never rebuilds the pipeline.\n      STORYBOOK_HEROES.actors.pixel.size = STORYBOOK_HEROES.actors.pixel.size === 1 ? 3 : 1;\n      if (this.current === 1) ctx.engine.setLook(STORYBOOK_HEROES);\n    }\n    ctx.hud.clear();\n    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, `LOOK ${this.current + 1}/${LOOKS.length}: ${LOOKS[this.current][0].toUpperCase()}`, { color: 'sand' });\n    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, '1-6 LOOKS  T CHUNKY', { anchor: 'bottom-left' });\n  }\n\n  cameraTarget() {\n    const p = this.model?.position ?? this.target.set(0, 0, 3);\n    return this.target.set(p.x, p.y + 0.9, p.z);\n  }\n\n  status() {\n    return `look ${LOOKS[this.current][0]}`;\n  }\n}\n\nawait Engine.start(new LookLab(), withUrlOptions({ container: document.getElementById('app'), camera: { preset: 'iso', zoom: 1.3 }, look: STORYBOOK_HEROES }));\n```\n\n## 6. Rules\n\n1. **Import everything from `./pixel-engine.js`.** three.js is `THREE` (`three/webgpu`) and\n   `TSL`; Rapier is `RAPIER`. Never load three.js or Rapier from a CDN or npm as well: two\n   copies break `instanceof` checks and the renderer.\n2. **Never edit `pixel-engine.js`.** Upgrade by replacing it (section 10).\n3. **No WebGL-era three.js APIs.** The renderer is `WebGPURenderer`: `WebGLRenderer`,\n   `EffectComposer`, `ShaderMaterial`, `onBeforeCompile` and GLSL don't work. Custom shading is\n   TSL on node materials; post effects are looks.\n4. **Gameplay in `fixedUpdate`, presentation in `update`.** Move characters and apply forces at\n   the fixed 60 Hz step; animate, spin pickups and draw the HUD per frame. Use `ctx.time`\n   (it stops while paused) and the `dt` you are given, never `performance.now()`. Anything\n   random comes from `ctx.random` (`range`, `int`, `chance`, `pick`, `fork`), never\n   `Math.random`: the same seed (`?seed=`) and input then replay the same game\n   (`engine.recording()`, `engine.replay()`, section 8). Draw it in `setup`, `fixedUpdate` and\n   event handlers; randomness drawn per frame in `update` (cosmetic) comes from a fork made in\n   `setup`, so the frame rate never changes what gameplay draws. Read buttons from `ctx.input`\n   (mouse buttons are keys: `isDown('MouseLeft')`; a finger is `Touch`), and data from the page (a save in storage, a\n   text field) through `ctx.input.send(data)` → `ctx.input.commands` next frame, so they replay too.\n5. **Physics owns positions.** Don't set `mesh.position` on things with bodies: `physics.bind`\n   them, or let the character controller pose its model. Static blocks: one collider each,\n   the same size as the mesh.\n6. **Palette colours and toon materials** (`toonMaterial(palette.x)`), chunky shapes (≥ 0.25 m).\n   Other materials render, but break the look.\n7. **Lights come from `ctx.lights`**, never `new PointLight()`. Dim `ctx.engine.sun` and\n   `ctx.engine.ambient` for night scenes.\n8. **Tag characters and objects** with `setLookLayer(root, 'actors')`, so looks can treat\n   them apart from the environment.\n9. **Reuse vectors in per-frame code** (`hero.feetInto(v)`, a `target` vector for\n   `cameraTarget`): no garbage per frame.\n10. **Give `status()` a meaningful line** (score, lives, level, state): the check tool and\n   other agents read it.\n11. **Clean up what the engine doesn't own**: DOM you add, timers and listeners go in `dispose(ctx)`.\n\n## 7. Common mistakes\n\n| Symptom | Cause | Fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Blank page, console: CORS / \"Failed to load module script\" | opened with `file://` | serve the folder over HTTP |\n| Console: \"Multiple instances of Three.js being imported\" | three.js loaded twice | use `THREE` from the bundle, nothing else |\n| A red **GAME ERROR** box | an exception in a hook | the box and `engine.errors` have the message, the console the stack: fix the first one |\n| \"Failed to start: ...\" | `setup()` threw (often a bad model path or a typo'd name) | read the message; check the path or the name in API.md |\n| The hero falls through the world | no collider under the mesh | `physics.addStaticBox` for every solid block |\n| The hero is stuck at the start | spawned inside a block | `position` is the feet: put it on the top surface |\n| A trigger never fires | wrong tag, or it doesn't overlap the body | `tag: 'character'` only matches the hero; level geometry needs `includeStatic: true` |\n| A colour is black or a material missing | a colour name that isn't in the palette | the 16 names in section 4 |\n| A model 404s | path not relative to the page | `assets/x.glb` next to `index.html`; built-ins need no file |\n| HUD text shows gaps | characters the 5×7 font doesn't have | uppercase letters, digits, common punctuation |\n| A key does nothing in `fixedUpdate` | `wasPressed` there | `consumePress` in `fixedUpdate`, `wasPressed` in `update` |\n| The ⚙ sits over the HUD's top-right corner on a phone | the settings button (on a mouse it shows only while the pointer moves) | `settings: { button: 'top-left' }`, or `false` and `engine.settings.open()` from your own menu |\n| Side-scroller hero drifts off the lane | `lockDepth` not passed | `lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth` |\n| The camera doesn't follow | `cameraTarget` returns nothing before `setup` ends | return a fallback vector (see the quick start) |\n| Slow, low fps | hundreds of separate meshes | `mergeStaticMeshes` the static level; share materials (`toonMaterial` caches) |\n| Everything is dark | a dimmed sun with no lights | `ctx.lights.request(...)` torches, or raise `ctx.engine.ambient.intensity` |\n| No sound | the browser waits for a key or tap | expected: sound starts on the first input |\n\n## 8. Debugging and checking\n\nThe engine is on `window.__PIXEL_ENGINE__` once it runs (also the value `Engine.start` returns).\nA tool waits on `window.__PIXEL_READY__` (it resolves with the engine, or rejects with why the start\nfailed); a game puts its own page handles in `ready(ctx)`, which runs then and after each `loadGame`.\n\n```js\nconst e = await window.__PIXEL_READY__;   // step() before this throws\ne.state();                // version, backend, fps, camera, look, game status, errors, gpuErrors, ...\ne.errors;                 // exceptions thrown by the game's hooks in the render loop (also on screen)\ne.step(60);               // manual time: exactly 60 frames (1 s), a hook's exception thrown to you; e.manual = false resumes\n                          // step() draws nothing, so a page screenshot still shows the old frame: read the new one with\n                          // await e.renderer.capture() (pixels, no HUD), or set e.manual = false and let a frame draw\ne.input.setKey('KeyD', true); e.step(30); e.input.setKey('KeyD', false);  // play by script\nconst rec = e.recording(); const end = e.fingerprint();            // this level's input as JSON, a hash of its state\nawait e.replay(rec, () => e.loadGame(new MyGame())); e.fingerprint() === end;  // replayed: the same game\n// Engine.headless(game, { assets }): the same game in Node (below)\nconst snap = await e.snapshot(); e.step(300); await e.restore(snap);   // a save-state (Game.snapshot / restore): back at once\nawait e.renderer.capture();   // { width, height, pixels }: the exact frame (without the HUD canvas)\ne.game;                   // your game object: e.game.hero.teleport([x, y, z]) to test a spot\ne.setLook(e.lookPresets.noir); e.setCamera({ preset: 'third' }, { syncUrl: false });\ne.audio.counts; e.particles.alive; e.physics.counts(); e.hud.canvas;\n```\n\nURL flags (with `withUrlOptions` in `Engine.start`): `?debug=1` (debug panel),\n`?look=noir`, `?filters=crt,scanlines`,\n`?camera=third`, `?zoom=1.5`, `?mode=raw` (no pixel pass), `?res=320`, `?touch=1`,\n`?quality=low`, `?fps=30`, `?seed=7` (`ctx.random`); they win over the player's saved settings\n(`e.settings.values`). Hotkeys, with `?debug=1`: P pixel/raw, R resolution, \\` debug panel, [ and ]\ncycle looks, M mute.\n\n**`check.mjs` plays a page in a real browser** and fails on anything wrong:\n\n```\nnode check.mjs                                  # index.html: start, run 90 frames, screenshot\nnode check.mjs --keys KeyD*60,Space*4,KeyD*40   # hold each key for that many frames, in order\nnode check.mjs examples/side-scroller.html --cameras --looks noir,handheld\nnode check.mjs \"index.html?look=handheld&zoom=1.5\"   # with URL flags\nnode check.mjs --sheets models.js               # sheets of your own models and textures (ASSETS.md)\n```\n\nIt plays on WebGPU in Chromium's headless shell, so it needs no display. It writes\n`check/<page>.png` (the frame with the HUD), the camera and look shots and\n`check/report.json` (errors, warnings, `state()`), and prints a summary: the backend, frames\nand your `status()`. Exit code 0: the game started, ran with no page, console, game or GPU\nerrors and drew a real picture; 1: it didn't; 2: the tool couldn't run (a browser without\nWebGPU, bad arguments, no page, no browser; `--help` lists the options). It needs Node 20.15+ (or 22.2+) and Playwright\n(`npm i -D playwright && npx playwright install chromium`; a global install works too), or\n`playwright-core` plus a Chromium in `CHROMIUM_PATH`. **Look at the PNGs**: a passing check only proves nothing crashed.\n\n**Play it in Node.** `Engine.headless(game, { assets })` runs a game as `Engine.start` does, minus\nthe GPU and DOM, on manual time, from the kit alone; `assets` reads the game's files.\n`node check.mjs --headless game.js` plays its `createGame()` (the starter's) for the `--keys`,\nreplays it in a fresh game and fails on an error or a replay that ends elsewhere. By hand:\n\n```js\n// test.mjs, beside game.js\nimport { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';\nimport { Engine } from './pixel-engine.js';\nimport { createGame } from './game.js';\n\nconst assets = async (path) => new Uint8Array(await readFile(new URL(path, import.meta.url))).buffer;\nconst engine = await Engine.headless(createGame(), { assets });\nengine.step(60); // input.setKey to play; a hook's error throws\nconst end = engine.fingerprint();\nawait engine.replay(engine.recording(), () => engine.loadGame(createGame()));\nconsole.log(engine.state().status, engine.fingerprint() === end);\n```\n\nCode that touches `document` guards it, as the starter does.\n\n## 9. Before you hand back\n\n- [ ] `node check.mjs` exits 0 for every page, with `--keys` that actually play the game\n      (reach a goal, collect something, lose a life).\n- [ ] `node check.mjs --headless game.js` exits 0 with the same `--keys`.\n- [ ] You looked at the screenshots: the scene reads, the HUD is legible, nothing is black or\n      clipped, the hero is on the ground.\n- [ ] `status()` reports the game's state, and it changes as you play.\n- [ ] No WebGL-era APIs, no second three.js, no `new PointLight`, no unknown palette names.\n- [ ] The controls are written down (the header comment of `game.js`, or on screen).\n- [ ] On a phone (or `?touch=1`) the game is playable with the touch buttons, or you set\n      `touchButtons` for its own actions.\n\n## 10. Versions\n\n`ENGINE_VERSION` (also `Engine.version` and `state().version`) is the engine's version, and\nthe kit's `CHANGELOG.md` says what changed in each. To upgrade a game, replace\n`pixel-engine.js` (and `pixel-engine.d.ts`, `types/` if you use them), read the changelog\nentries since the old version, then run `node check.mjs` again. The newest release of the kit is\nat the URL in `manifest.json`'s `latest`, and every release (from 0.39.0, its changelog as the\nnotes) stays downloadable from `releases`. The kit's `README.md` says how to build\nit from the engine's repository (`source`) instead.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '25,105p' ASSETS.md && sed -n '231,430p' vendor/README.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '25,105p' ASSETS.md && sed -n '231,430p' vendor/README.md\"","aggregated_output":"   imports it. Keep the engine start out of that module.\n3. **Check and look:** `node check.mjs --sheets models.js` draws a sheet of every def the module\n   exports (by name, in an array or object, or in a catalog: `check/sheets/<id>.png`; textures\n   `<id>.tex.png`) and prints its problems and warnings; it exits 1 on a problem. **Open the\n   PNGs.** `--sheets fence,cobbles` draws library ids: look at one before you place it.\n4. **Fix** the numbers the messages and the sheet point at, and run it again until there is no\n   problem and the game-scale previews read. Warnings name what won't read at 480×270: fix them\n   unless you mean it.\n5. **Look in the game:** `node check.mjs index.html --cameras`, then the PNGs: the real\n   renderer, with lights and looks.\n\nIn code, `checkModel(def, { catalog })` and `validateTexture(def)` return\n`{ problems, warnings }`, and `modelSheet(def, { catalog })` and `textureSheet(def)` return the\nsheet as RGBA pixels (`image`) with them.\n\n| sheet row | answers |\n| --- | --- |\n| turntable, 8 angles at one scale | Is the silhouette right from every side? |\n| front / side / top on a 0.1 m grid, with the size in metres | Are the parts where the numbers say? Do they touch? Is it the right size? |\n| game scale: an iso camera (yaw 45°, pitch 42°) at about 19 and 22 pixels a metre, one art pixel per pixel, ×4 | Does it read in a game? (The engine's iso camera at zoom 1 shows about 20.) |\n| palette, check | Which slots, which colours, which are unused; the problems and warnings, each naming the part and the fix |\n\nSheets are drawn in software (three shading bands, outlines) without point lights or looks: the\ngame is the truth.\n\n## 2. Models\n\n```js\nexport const crate = model({\n  id: 'crate',\n  about: 'Wooden crate with a frame and diagonal braces', // one line: find() searches it\n  tags: ['container', 'wood'],\n  palette: { wood: 'orange', band: 'red' }, // slots → the 16 palette names (GUIDE.md section 4)\n  parts: [\n    { box: [0.68, 0.68, 0.68], at: [0, 0.36, 0], c: 'wood' },\n    boxFrame({ size: [0.72, 0.72, 0.72], beam: 0.09, at: [0, 0.36, 0], c: 'band' }),\n    { mirror: [{ box: [0.8, 0.08, 0.04], at: [0, 0.36, 0.35], rot: [0, 0, 45], c: 'band' }], axis: 'z' },\n  ],\n});\n```\n\nMetres and degrees; +Y is up, +Z is the front, the floor is y = 0. Parts are keyed, never\npositional. **Every primitive fills its box `[w, h, d]`, centred on `at`**, turned by `rot` (XYZ,\nabout `at`), and round kinds stretch to it:\n\n- `box`; `cyl` (an n-sided prism, `seg` sides, default 6 to 10 by size; `top`: the top width ÷\n  the bottom width); `cone` (point up); `ball` (`half`: the upper dome); `lump` (a rock, `seed`);\n  `wedge` (a ramp, full height at the back, −Z); `torus` (a ring in XZ as thick as the box is tall).\n- Profile kinds use their own coordinates, placed at `at`: `lathe` ((radius, height) points\n  turned about Y), `slab` (an XY outline or SVG path, extruded `depth` along Z), `tube` (points\n  and a radius `r`, or a pair that tapers).\n- Every part takes `c` (its slot; default the first), `name`, `glow: true` (unlit: a flame's\n  heart, eyes), `tex` (a texture id, section 3), `seg` and `collide: false` (no box of its own\n  under `collider: 'parts'`: a doorway's mortar ring).\n\n**Composition:**\n\n- `{ group: [...], at, rot, scale, c }`: a frame whose `c` is its parts' default. `pivot: true`\n  with a `name` keeps it a movable Group (a door, a lid: `build.object.getObjectByName('lid')`).\n- `{ mirror: [...], axis: 'x' }`: the parts and their mirror image (`axis: 'z'` mirrors front to back).\n- `{ use: 'barrel', at, rot, scale, params, seed, palette: { theirs: 'ours' } }`: another model\n  by id. Its slots map onto yours through `palette`, else onto yours of the same name, else keep\n  their own colours.\n- `on: 'name'`: sit on the named part's top; `at` becomes an offset from there.\n- `params: { h: [default, min, max] }` with `parts: (p, rng) => [...]`: parametric (`p.h`) and\n  seeded (`rng.range(a, b)`). Helpers return parts: `around`, `rowOf`, `gridOf`, `scatterOf`,\n  `boxFrame`.\n- Model keys: `size` (declared bounds, checked), `anchors` (`{ grip: [x, y, z] }`), `light: { at,\n  c, intensity, radius, flicker }`, `collider` (`'box'`, `'cyl'`, `'hull'`, `'parts'` (a box per\n  part: doorways stay open) or `'none'`), `floats: true` (it hangs), `texel` (texels a metre for\n  `tex` parts, default 8).\n\n**Problems** (`check.mjs --sheets` exits 1; `buildModel` throws a `ModelError` on those it can't\nbuild): no `about` or `tags`; an undeclared slot or a colour that isn't a palette name; an unknown\n`use`, `on` or texture; a size that isn't positive; `seg` outside 3–64; NaN; an empty model; below\nthe floor (unless `floats`); over the declared `size`; params out of range; a part that both\nglows and takes a `tex`; a `texel` outside 2–64. **Warnings:** a part floating more than 1 cm from the\nrest; rods under 0.06 m; over the triangle budget (300 under 0.5 m across, 1,000 under 2 m, else\n3,000); over 8 materials; a part hidden inside another; coplanar faces of two colours; touching big\nmasses too close in lightness; one colour over 40% of the model in four or more parts; glow over\n15% of it. `checkModel` builds the defaults, every param at its min and at its max, and three\n  that player leaving.\n\n### Scoring\n\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\n  progress.\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\n  result instead of medals.\n- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\n\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\nresults:\n\n- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\n- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\n\nPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\n\n---\n\n## Sending data between copies\n\n### Parallel\n\nThe copies send nothing to each other. `send()` is not used.\n\n### Ghost\n\nEach copy sends its own player's position and what the player is doing to\nthe other copies with `send()`. Each copy shows the other players' characters\nonly while they are in the same area of the game as its own player, and those\ncharacters do not affect its world. `Math.random` is seeded as in parallel.\n\n### Shared world\n\n- The host's copy (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the world. The other copies send the\n  host what their own player does, and show the world the host sends them.\n- The hub can run the host's copy with no player of its own: `AIHub.server`\n  is `true` and `AIHub.me` is not among `AIHub.players`. The host's copy runs\n  the world in that case too, with no local player, camera or input.\n- `Math.random` is not seeded. `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on\n  every copy.\n\n### `send()` and `message`\n\n- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends `data` to every other copy, or\n  to the one player `to`. It returns `false` when the message is dropped.\n- `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` receives it; `from` is the\n  sender's player id. A copy never receives its own messages. Messages sent\n  before another copy has loaded are queued for it.\n- `data` is JSON, at most 16 KB.\n- At most 20 messages a second leave each copy, with bursts up to 40; the\n  client drops the rest.\n- Reliable messages (the default) arrive in the order they were sent.\n  Messages sent with `reliable: false` may be lost or arrive out of order. An\n  unreliable message larger than about 1,200 bytes travels as several packets\n  and is lost whole if any of them is.\n\n---\n\n## Rules\n\nIn a session:\n\n- **Network only through the hub.** No WebSocket, WebRTC, or `fetch` to a\n  game server or public broker. Loading the game's own files is fine.\n- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\n  names. Names come from the hub.\n- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\n  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\n  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\n- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\n  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\n  is held.\n\nEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\none, the game is not affected.\n\n---\n\n## Test it\n\nThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\nand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\nto the game:\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\npython3 -m http.server 8000\n```\n\nThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\nThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\n`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\n`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\n`&players=2&scenario=server`.\n\nThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\n`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\n`\"true\"` at the end. It checks:\n\n- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\n- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\n- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\n  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\n- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\n  player of its own;\n- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\n  told the round ended;\n- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\n\n**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\ncases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\n`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\ngame is served from the harness's own origin.\n\nIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\nchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\nruns there.\n\n---\n\n## Reference\n\n### Tags\n\n| Tag | |\n|---|---|\n| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\n| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\n| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\n| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\n| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\n\n### API\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\n| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\n| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\n| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\n| `AIHub.result({ time \\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\n| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\n| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\n| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\n| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\n| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\n\n`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\nread them.\n\n| Event | Arguments |\n|---|---|\n| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\n| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\n| `message` | `data, from` |\n| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\n| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\n| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\n\nEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\norder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\nreach the game through the client unchanged.\n\n### Versions\n\nThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\ngames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\nprotocol 2, with its own `hub:session` value.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '665,905p' PLAN.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '665,905p' PLAN.md\"","aggregated_output":"- `or-calm`, `or-tense` and `or-critical`: same key and tempo family so switching at bar lines sounds intentional.\n- Four boss themes with saw \"choir\" pads. Dies Irae's tempo follows the heart: restart with new `bpm` at bar lines every few steps.\n- Op 9's silence, then a swell. Op 10 sparse and gentle. Results fanfare and sad variant. Credits.\n\n`MusicDirector` changes tracks at bar lines from vitals and stage. `loadGame` clears music and loops, so have the director restart on each stage.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Architecture\n\n```\npixel-engine.js check.mjs GUIDE.md API.md …   the kit, unchanged, as it unzips\nindex.html, game.js          game.js exports createGame() and starts the engine only when `document` exists\nsrc/core/     config.js (defaults + live values), rng.js, save.js, meta.js (title, tagline)\nsrc/sim/      op.js, tools.js, entities.js, strains/*.js, scoring.js, bot.js, codec.js, campaign.js\nsrc/content/  ops/*.js, scenes/*.js, lines.js, cast.js, arenas.js, songs.js, sfx.js, icons.js, about.js, models/*.js\nsrc/view/     field.js, hands.js, strains/*.js, sets.js, bust.js, figure.js, fx.js\nsrc/ui/       hud.js, prompts.js, menu.js, then one file per screen\nsrc/stages/   title.js story.js briefing.js operation.js results.js log.js consult.js card.js attract.js\nsrc/input/    devices.js, cursor.js\nsrc/net/      session.js, host.js, client.js\nsrc/audio/    voice.js, director.js\nvendor/       session.js, harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (from the hub, unmodified)\ntools/build.mjs\ntests/        run.mjs, *.test.mjs, browser/*, balance.mjs\ndocs/AUTHORING.md            how to add an op, scene, strain or character\n```\n\n**The one rule that makes everything else possible: `src/sim` and non-model content import nothing from the engine, the DOM or the clock.** The sim is plain data plus pure functions: `sim = createOp(def, { seed, seats, difficulty, config })`, `step(sim, inputsBySeat)` at 60 Hz, `events = drainEvents(sim)`.\n\n- Input per seat is `{ x, z, tool, use, gift, steady, drop, … }`.\n- Randomness comes from its own seeded generator, never `Math.random`.\n- State must survive `structuredClone`. This one rule gives you:\n  - The Second Look.\n  - Determinism: same seed plus inputs gives the same hash.\n  - Tests in Node in milliseconds.\n  - The bot.\n  - A hub server copy with no renderer.\n  - Network snapshots.\n\n**Stages.** Each screen is a `Game` class, swapped with `engine.loadGame(new XStage(args))` under `engine.screen.cover/reveal`. A persistent `App` singleton holds save, settings, devices, config and the director. Set `status()` meaningfully (`op5 stage=2 vitals=64 t=121 score=3400`) and put test handles in `ready(ctx)` (`window.__GAME__ = { app, sim, goto, config }`). In solo, step the sim from `fixedUpdate`; draw in `update`. Cosmetic randomness comes from `ctx.random.fork('fx')`.\n\n**Content is data**, so the rest of the game can be built with ordinary tools (JSON-safe, hot-editable):\n\n```js\nexport default { id:'op1', title:'ELEVEN HOOKS', arena:'forearm', surgeon:'ilya', gifts:[], tools:['drain','forceps','suture','gel','syringe'],\n  patient:{ name:'TOMASZ DOLAN', age:52, job:'TRAWLER DECKHAND', hook:\"HIS DAUGHTER'S WEDDING IS SATURDAY.\",\n            followUp:{ S:'He walked her down the aisle. Dry eyes.', C:'He danced badly. She cried anyway.' } },\n  time:240, vitals:{ start:85, drain:.10 }, doses:3, music:'or-calm', par:4200,\n  stages:[\n    { id:'pools', say:'reyes.drainFirst', hint:'drain',\n      spawn:[{ k:'pool', id:'p1', at:[-3,.6], r:1.6 }, { k:'hook', id:'h1', at:[-3,.9], hiddenBy:'p1', arc:.8, pull:1.2 }],\n      until:{ clear:['pool'] },\n      on:{ vitalsBelow:{ 50:{ say:'reyes.stabilize', unlock:'syringe' } } } },\n    …\n  ] };\n```\n\n- Entity kinds (`pool, hook, nick, laceration, incision, tumor, polyp, prop, magnet, bleeder, clamp, vessel, fragment, organ, mask, strain:*`) register their tool handlers in `entities.js`.\n- `until` takes `clear`, `flag`, `all` or `time`. Commands: `say, spawn, remove, unlock, setDrain, music, camera, flag, codeBlue`.\n- Scenes are data too: `{ id, set, music, beats:[{ who, emo, say, cam, cue }] }`. Reaction pools live in `lines.js` (`reyes.mistake[]`, `reyes.clean[]`, `reyes.lowVitals[]`).\n\n**The bot** (`src/sim/bot.js`) reads sim state and produces seat input, with `skill` 0 to 1 scaling speed, path noise, reaction delay and judgement (syringe when low, gifts when useful). It knows a strategy per entity kind. It is one piece of code with four jobs: **attract mode, the whole-game test playthrough, the balance report and the Op 9 partner.** Build it early.\n\n**Engine facts to save you time**\n\n- ✔ `update()` does not run while `engine.paused` is true; it does run at `timeScale = 0`.\n- `loadGame` clears HUD, music, loops, particles and lights; it keeps Input, volumes and look.\n- `engine.settings.open()` is a mouse-driven DOM panel that pauses the engine: use it only on the touch-only path; build your own Settings.\n- Start options: `aspect:'fill'`, `touch:false`, `settings:{ button:false, key: inSession ? false : '<slug>' }`, `background`, plus `withUrlOptions` for review flags.\n- Saves: guarded `localStorage` under a versioned key. In a session the client hands you an empty store.\n- `game.js` must import cleanly in Node for `check.mjs --headless`: guard `document`, `navigator.getGamepads` and `localStorage`.\n- Own URL flags outside a session, for tests: `?op= &stage= &diff= &seed= &bot= &god= &attract= &dev= &scene=`.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Balance\n\n**Dev Mode sliders (live, with Reset to Defaults).** All in `config.js`:\n\n- vitals drain ×, bleed drain, mistake penalty, max mistakes, time limit ×\n- cursor speed, steady factor, aim assist, tool tolerance ×\n- drain rate, laser burn time, syringe dose\n- Composure gain per CLEAN, Quiet slow factor, Quiet drain, Second Look cost and seconds\n- strain speed ×, strain HP ×, Dies Irae base BPM\n- Code Blue zone width, co-op scale per seat\n\n**`node tests/balance.mjs`** runs each op on the bot at skills 0.4, 0.7 and 1.0 and prints time used, final vitals, mistakes and rank. Targets on Resident:\n\n| Bot skill | Should | |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 (idle, no input) | fail every op | |\n| 0.4 | clear Ops 1 to 3, may lose bosses | a nervous beginner |\n| 0.7 | clear all, mostly B or A, at least 20% clock left outside bosses | a decent player |\n| 1.0 | S on all | sets `par` |\n\n---\n\n## 13. Testing\n\nOne command: `node tests/run.mjs` prints only failures and one count line. An optional filter (`sim`, `content`, `browser`, `hub`) lets you rerun only what you changed.\n\n**Node (fast, most tests):**\n\n- Each tool's rules, success and mistake paths.\n- Every op: idle fails; bot 0.7 clears; determinism hash stable; retry-from-stage works.\n- Code Blue paths, The Quiet's time scale, The Second Look's exact restore.\n- Scoring and rank bounds.\n- Slider apply and reset.\n- Codec round trip and sizes at each op's peak.\n- **Whole-game scripted playthrough:** `campaign.js` driven by the bot through every scene and op to credits, with save and unlock checks.\n\n**Content lint:**\n\n- Cast and sets referenced exist; every tool used; all text fits two wrapped lines at 342 art pixels wide; every character is in the font's set; glyph tokens valid.\n- No `TODO` or placeholder strings; About has every field.\n- No forbidden source names.\n- `check.mjs --sheets` clean of problems.\n\n**Browser (Chromium, one or two at a time):** launch args ✔ `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader --autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required`.\n\nSoftware WebGPU runs at 5 to 30 fps here; prefer the sim's state and `engine.step()` over waiting, and use a 960×540 viewport for logic. A pad stub:\n```js\nwindow.__pad = { connected:true, mapping:'standard', index:0, id:'stub', axes:[0,0,0,0], buttons:Array.from({length:17},()=>({pressed:false,value:0})), vibrationActuator:{ playEffect:()=>Promise.resolve('complete') } };\nnavigator.getGamepads = () => [window.__pad, null, null, null];   // add_init_script\n```\nCover:\n\n- The built page from `file://`: boots, no errors. Outside a session `AIHub.active` is false and `AIHub.seeded` is not true. Enter, Space, Enter, Space then still draws (the hub's own \"on its own\" check).\n- Fresh profile: music slider 0.1.\n- Menu navigation by pad stub, keys and mouse (hover moves focus).\n- Prompts switch with the last device; the Xbox option swaps glyphs.\n- A real drain and forceps action by mouse and by pad stub (the sim state changes at the aimed spot).\n- Attract: idle 10 s starts it; key and pad each exit; save untouched.\n- Touch-only with `p.devices['Pixel 7']`: the exact text, the ⚙, no joystick, attract runs.\n- Pause, then Dev Mode: a slider changes live config; reset restores it.\n- Couch co-op with two stub pads: two independent tips.\n- `engine.errors` and `gpuErrors` empty throughout.\n\n**Sound:** render syllables and `SoundDef`s (`renderSound`) in Node: non-silent, finite. In the page, `audio.counts` rise while talking. An `AnalyserNode` level check on one voice line.\n\n**Hub:** copy `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` beside the built page in `dist/`. Serve **`python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`**. ✔ The `-u` matters: when you read the port from a pipe without it, the line never arrives and your script hangs (this cost me about fifteen minutes). Stop that server by its PID. Run these with launch args plus `--no-sandbox`, a 1600×1000 viewport, and wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'`:\n```\nharness.html?game=game.html&auto=1&players=2\nharness.html?game=game.html&auto=1&players=3&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5\nharness.html?game=game.html&auto=1&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5\nharness.html?game=game.html&auto=1&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&scenario=host-leave\nharness.html?game=game.html&auto=1&players=2&scenario=server\n```\nAll must give `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`.\n\n**Kit tools** ✔ all work here after `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in the dev folder (about 1 to 3 s each): `node check.mjs` (serves on an OS-picked 127.0.0.1 port), `node check.mjs --headless game.js --keys …`, `node check.mjs --sheets …`.\n\n**Screenshots only for what scripts can't judge**, at 1920×1080: each arena, each set, each bust, title, results, HUD readability. Look at them. Blank WebGPU canvases can happen; ✔ here `page.screenshot` captured every frame fine.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Build and packaging\n\n`tools/build.mjs` ✔ (this exact recipe gave a 6 MB page that booted from `file://` in about 1 s on software WebGPU):\n\n```js\nexecFileSync('esbuild', ['game.js','--bundle','--format=esm','--platform=browser','--target=es2022','--outfile=dist/bundle.js']);\nconst js = readFileSync('dist/bundle.js','utf8').replace(/<\\/script/gi,'<\\\\/script');\nconst session = readFileSync('vendor/session.js','utf8');           // verbatim: the hub compares it byte for byte\nconst pack = readFileSync('packs/kenney-furniture.json').toString('base64');\nconst html = `<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<script>\\n${session}\\n</script>\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>${TITLE} — ${TAGLINE}</title> …description, theme-color, data-URL icon, hub:* tags…\n</head><body><div id=\"app\"></div>\n<script>window.__PIXEL_ASSETS__={'packs/kenney-furniture.json':'data:application/json;base64,${pack}'};</script>\n<script type=\"module\">\\n${js}\\n</script></body></html>`;\n```\n\n- The output name comes from your brief: one constant at the top of the script. Write `dist/game.html` for the tests and a copy where your brief wants the deliverable.\n- Don't minify the HTML or touch the session client's text.\n- Also add `hub:type` Game, `hub:source` for the source, and an emblem icon as a data URL.\n- When you report research, say this plan's planner read the source's Wikipedia page, the hub's protocol and the engine kit, and ran engine spikes; add anything you did yourself.\n- For the exit-interview file your brief asks for, `aiHub.genre` is `Simulation`, `players` `1–4`, `series` `Trauma Center`. Good tags: `3d`, `pixel art`, `story`, `co-op`, `boss fights`, `comedy`.\n\n---\n\n## 15. Order of work\n\nBuild in this order. Keep the game running and the tests green after each step.\n\n1. **Skeleton.** Unzip the kit and move the contents of its `pixel-engine/` folder to the dev folder root. `npm install playwright@1.63.0` there. Then: build script, test runner, an empty stage booting from `file://`, music default, input devices with pad stub test.\n2. **One great op.** Sim core, tissue canvas, hand and tools, HUD, Drain, Forceps, Suture, Gel, Syringe, Op 1 playable on pad, mouse and keys. Make this feel wonderful before moving on.\n3. **All tools and the bot.** Scalpel, Laser, Scanner; Ops 2 and 3; Code Blue; The Quiet; bot and balance report; whole-op tests.\n4. **The drama.** Busts, figures, sets, story stage, voices, sound effects, music director, results, saves. Eps 1 to 3 flow end to end.\n5. **The shell.** Title, menus, prompts, settings, pause, Dev Mode, attract, touch gate, About.\n6. **Bosses and the rest.** Second Look, Bone Mend, transplant, Introit, Lacrimosa, Dies Irae, Libera Me, Do No Harm, all scenes, campaign playthrough test.\n7. **Consult.** Couch first (it proves the seats), then session, card, net, harness passes.\n8. **Polish and balance.** Juice, feel, per-arena art, tune against the targets, full screenshot pass at 1080p, `docs/AUTHORING.md`.\n9. **Finish.** Fill in About and your chat reply and exit-interview file as your brief says.\n\n**If you run short, cut whole content rather than leave anything half-made** (the brief forbids stubs). Cut in this order:\n\n1. The `RenderView` and `Mirror` touches.\n2. Extra session fights (keep Pile-Up).\n3. Op 3's third table.\n4. Lacrimosa's crawling phase.\n5. Op 6's cold-time bar.\n\nNever cut: Ops 1, 2, 5, 8, 9; both gifts; Code Blue; attract; Dev Mode; hub conformance; couch co-op.\n\n## 16. Done means\n\n| The brief asks for | Where |\n|---|---|\n| Genre and perspective chosen | §1 |\n| Title by the brief's method | Yours, `src/core/meta.js` |\n| One HTML, engine unmodified, session client verbatim | §14 |\n| Native art plus new assets | §9 |\n| Native animation plus new animations | §6, §9 (`HERO_CLIPS` on custom rigs, procedural tools) |\n| All audio in code, gibberish voices, music at 10% | §10 |\n| AI Hub integration and the game on its own | §7, §13 |\n| Feature-complete demo showing every mechanic | §3, §5, §6 |\n| World, characters, themes, tone, writing style | §2 |\n| Infrastructure for more content with normal tools | §11, `docs/AUTHORING.md`, Dev Mode's Copy Op JSON |\n| Dev mode from pause, live sliders, reset | §8, §12 |\n| Attract after 10 s, any input exits | §8 |\n| Controller-first at 10 feet, native keyboard and mouse, prompts by last input, Xbox option | §4, §8 |\n| No dense data screens | §8 |\n| Touch-only gate, settings button, attract runs | §8 |\n| Tests behind one command, whole-game playthrough | §13 |\n| No TODOs or placeholders | §13 lint |\n| Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback in chat and About; exit interview | §8, §14, your brief |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
